# Nuclear 03 — Radiometric dating: carbon-14 and a Rb–Sr isochron
#
# (Krane, "Introductory Nuclear Physics", §6.7.)
#  (a) Carbon dating.  Living carbon shows 15.3 C-14 decays per minute per
#      gram (t½ = 5730 yr).  A 5.00 g charcoal sample from an ancient fire
#      pit shows 32.0 decays per minute.  How old is it, and how many C-14
#      atoms are left in it?
#  (b) Rb–Sr isochron.  Rb-87 decays to Sr-87 with λ = 1.3972×10⁻¹¹ per year.
#      Minerals that formed together with different Rb contents lie today on
#      the straight line
#          ⁸⁷Sr/⁸⁶Sr = (⁸⁷Sr/⁸⁶Sr)₀ + (e^{λt} − 1) · ⁸⁷Rb/⁸⁶Sr.
#      Fit the ten meteorite minerals in data/rb_sr_isochron.csv (simulated
#      for this problem) and find the age and the initial ratio.

# (a)
t_half = 5730 yr
λ_C = ln(2) / t_half
A_modern = 15.3 min⁻¹ g⁻¹
A_sample = 32.0 min⁻¹
mass = 5.00 g
age = ln(A_modern mass / A_sample) / λ_C
print "(a) age =", age in yr to 8 digits
print "    C-14 atoms =", A_sample / λ_C to 8 digits

# (b)
λ_Rb = 1.3972e-11 yr⁻¹
data = load "data/rb_sr_isochron.csv"
fit Sr87_Sr86 = slope Rb87_Sr86 + ratio0 to data with slope = 0.07, ratio0 = 0.7
print "(b) slope =", slope to 8 digits, "  initial ratio =", ratio0 to 8 digits
print "    age =", ln(1 + slope) / λ_Rb in Gyr to 8 digits
